<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Ali al-Abid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Ali al-Abid was the first president of the Syrian Republic under the French mandate, serving from 1932 to 1936. The son of Izzat Pasha al-Abid, a senior adviser of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a>, he was a wealthy Damascene notable acceptable both to the French and to moderate nationalists. He resigned in 1936 as the National Bloc negotiated a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-al-abid.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ali al-Abid" width="289" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Ali_al-Abid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad Ali al-Abid was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. His father, Ahmad Izzat al-Abid, worked in the administration of Damascus Vilayet and founded a periodical, then in 1887 moved with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where Muhammad Ali attended Galatasaray High School. He went on to study law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>, graduating in 1905. From 1894 his father was an adviser of the sultan, head of his intelligence services and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>.</p><p>Fluent in Arabic, French and Turkish, with a good knowledge of English and Persian, al-Abid entered the foreign affairs administration and in 1907 became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s ambassador to Washington. He returned soon after the constitution was declared on 23 July 1908, when the Young Turks revolted against the sultan. His father fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and al-Abid travelled with him through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on the eve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Al-Abid moved back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in the summer of 1920, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> came under the French Mandate, and in 1922 he became Minister of Finance. Elected to the parliament in Damascus as a nominee of the National Bloc on 30 April 1932, he was raised on 11 June to the presidency, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had granted Syria partial recognition of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. He was made a Commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour in 1932. After a free parliament was elected in 1936 he resigned and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Hashim al-Atasi, the nominee of the National Bloc, replaced him, though French troops did not leave Syria completely until 1946.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Abid died of a heart attack at a hotel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 22 October 1939, a few months after Bahij al-Khatib had become head of state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His body was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and on 16 November his funeral procession left from there for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In French he spelled his own name Mehmed Ali Abed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Ali Bey al-Abid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Muhammad Ali al-Abid</b> became its first president in 1932.</blockquote><a href="/state/syrian-republic">Syrian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad Ali al-Abid"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5448 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5448 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.51,36.29&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
